00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:03.000
Let's start by separating
two related ideas:

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:06.930
AI and generative AI.

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:10.890
AI imitates human behavior
by using machine learning

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:14.610
to interact with its
environment and execute tasks

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:19.710
without being explicitly
told exactly what the output

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:21.060
should be.

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:24.780
Generative AI is a
specific type of AI

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:27.330
that creates original content.

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:31.710
This is what powers many
modern chat application.

00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:35.910
Generative AI application
takes natural language input

00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:40.020
and return responses
in different format.

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:43.830
One common format is natural
language generation.

00:03:43.830 --> 00:03:47.910
For example, you might
ask give me three ideas

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:50.910
for healthy breakfast,
including peppers,

00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:54.090
and receive a clear,
written answer.

00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:56.520
Another format is
image generation,

00:03:56.520 --> 00:03:59.130
where requests might
create an image

00:03:59.130 --> 00:04:04.080
of an elephant eating a burger
can result in a new image.

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:07.890
A third format is code
generation, where you might ask,

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:12.240
show me how to code a game
of tic-tac-toe with Python,

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:16.740
and the tool produces starter
code that you can refine.

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:21.780
A popular example of this kind
of application is ChatGPT,

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:26.370
a chat bot created by OpenAI,
an AI research company

00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:29.310
that partners closely
with Microsoft.

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:34.740
The key point is that generative
AI isn't just automation.

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:40.270
It's content creation driven
by language understanding.

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:44.150
Generative AI
application are powered

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:47.630
by large language
models, or LLMs.

00:04:47.630 --> 00:04:50.960
These are specialized machine
learning models designed

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:53.820
for natural language
processing tasks

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:56.700
that includes
determining sentiment

00:04:56.700 --> 00:05:01.380
or classifying text more
broadly, summarizing text,

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:05.550
comparing multiple text sources
for semantic similarities,

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:09.180
and generating new
natural language.

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:13.230
Even though the math behind
LLM can get complex,

00:05:13.230 --> 00:05:16.710
having a basic
conceptual understanding

00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:20.850
of how they're built and
used makes it easier

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:24.450
to understand why Copilots
can be so helpful,

00:05:24.450 --> 00:05:29.400
and why the way you ask
questions has a direct impact

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:33.060
on the quality of the output.

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:36.630
Now let's connect those
models to the cloud platform

00:05:36.630 --> 00:05:39.840
where many organization
deploy them.

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:44.670
Azure OpenAI service is
Microsoft's cloud solution

00:05:44.670 --> 00:05:50.010
for deploying, customizing, and
hosting large language models.

00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:54.540
It brings together OpenAI
models and API with security,

00:05:54.540 --> 00:06:00.570
scalability, and governance
you expect from Azure.

00:06:00.570 --> 00:06:06.120
Azure OpenAI services include
pretrained generative AI models,

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:10.320
customization capabilities,
built-in tool to detect

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:13.320
and mitigate harmful use cases

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:17.340
so you can implement
AI responsibly,

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:21.420
an enterprise scale security
like role-based access control,

00:06:21.420 --> 00:06:25.200
RBAC, and support
for private network.

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:27.090
And when it comes
to development,

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:31.230
you can build solution
using Azure AI Foundry,

00:06:31.230 --> 00:06:37.410
the REST-API-supported
SDKs, or the Azure CLI.

00:06:37.410 --> 00:06:40.740
Azure OpenAI supports
multiple-use cases

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:42.780
to serve different needs.

00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:46.140
GPT-4o models are the
latest generation

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:49.140
for natural language
and code completion.

00:06:49.140 --> 00:06:54.180
GPT-3.5 models like GPT-3.5
Turbo are optimized

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:57.000
for chat-based
interaction and work well

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:00.180
with many generative
AI solution.

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:03.900
Embedding model converts
text into numerical vectors,

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:07.170
which helps with
language-analytic tasks

00:07:07.170 --> 00:07:10.230
like comparing text
for similarities.

00:07:10.230 --> 00:07:13.770
And finally, we also have
DALL-E models, which are used

00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:18.660
for image generation from
natural language prompts.

00:07:18.660 --> 00:07:22.920
Developers can work with these
models in Azure AI Studio,

00:07:22.920 --> 00:07:27.090
a web-based environment
where you can deploy, test,

00:07:27.090 --> 00:07:32.460
and manage the models used for
generative AI apps deployment.

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:36.300
This is where you can
experiment in Chat Playground,

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:39.090
provide few-shot
example, and validate

00:07:39.090 --> 00:07:45.390
that the model behavior matches
what your application needs.

00:07:45.390 --> 00:07:48.360
The availability of large
language model has led

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:54.570
to a new category of computing
often referred to as Copilots.

00:07:54.570 --> 00:07:56.730
Copilots are
typically integrated

00:07:56.730 --> 00:08:01.410
in many existing application and
provide help with common tasks

00:08:01.410 --> 00:08:05.370
by tapping into
generative AI models.

00:08:05.370 --> 00:08:08.640
You've probably seen
this pattern already

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:13.140
where a chat panel open next to
your work and helps you draft,

00:08:13.140 --> 00:08:17.880
summarize, or create
content using context

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:20.340
from the product
you're already in.

00:08:20.340 --> 00:08:24.900
It's also useful to think about
the life cycle behind a Copilot.

00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:28.830
First, huge amount
of data are used

00:08:28.830 --> 00:08:31.500
to train a large language model.

00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:36.120
Then services like Azure OpenAI
make those pretrained models

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:40.350
available, and developers
can use them as is,

00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:44.190
or fine-tune them
with custom data.

00:08:44.190 --> 00:08:45.990
Once a model is deployed,

00:08:45.990 --> 00:08:48.870
it becomes available
for applications.

00:08:48.870 --> 00:08:53.340
Developers then build Copilot
experiences that submit prompts

00:08:53.340 --> 00:08:58.110
to the model and shape the
output of real workflow.

00:08:58.110 --> 00:09:01.680
And finally, business
users use those Copilots

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:05.310
to boost productivity
and creativity,

00:09:05.310 --> 00:09:09.570
helping with first draft,
synthesizing information,

00:09:09.570 --> 00:09:13.480
supporting planning,
and so much more.

00:09:15.090 --> 00:09:17.100
Next, let's focus on something

00:09:17.100 --> 00:09:19.650
that directly affects
your day-to-day success

00:09:19.650 --> 00:09:23.370
with any Copilot:
prompt engineering.

00:09:23.370 --> 00:09:26.760
Prompt engineering is the
process of creating prompts

00:09:26.760 --> 00:09:31.200
to get better outcomes, and
it isn't only for developers.

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:36.060
Anyone using a generative
AI application can improve

00:09:36.060 --> 00:09:42.300
responses quality by using clear
language setting expectation,

00:09:42.300 --> 00:09:44.340
providing examples,

00:09:44.340 --> 00:09:48.450
and grounding the requests
in relevant data.

00:09:48.450 --> 00:09:52.320
One technique is direct
language, being explicit

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:54.570
about the type of
response you want.

00:09:54.570 --> 00:09:58.410
For example, create a
list of 10 things to do

00:09:58.410 --> 00:10:01.410
in Edinburgh during August.

00:10:01.410 --> 00:10:05.040
Another technique is
using system messages,

00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:09.330
where you describe how the
assistant should pass,

00:10:09.330 --> 00:10:13.170
such as you're a helpful
assistant that responds

00:10:13.170 --> 00:10:16.320
in a cheerful, friendly manner.

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:19.320
You also provide example.

00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:22.860
Even though LLMs can respond
with zero-shot learning,

00:10:22.860 --> 00:10:27.510
meaning without example, you
can get more consistent result

00:10:27.510 --> 00:10:30.510
with one-shot or
few-shot learning

00:10:30.510 --> 00:10:35.580
by giving it a sample response
like visit the castle

00:10:35.580 --> 00:10:38.460
in the morning before
the cloud arrive,

00:10:38.460 --> 00:10:42.630
and letting the model
continue with that style.

00:10:42.630 --> 00:10:45.990
Finally, grounding data
gives the model context

00:10:45.990 --> 00:10:50.190
so that it can generate
a more accurate output.

00:10:50.190 --> 00:10:54.200
For instance, if you want
a summary of an email,

00:10:54.200 --> 00:10:56.120
include the email
text in a prompt

00:10:56.120 --> 00:10:59.180
and ask the model
to summarize it.

00:10:59.180 --> 00:11:03.530
Overall, the quality of a
response depends not only

00:11:03.530 --> 00:11:08.250
on the model, but also how
you communicate your goal

00:11:08.250 --> 00:11:10.890
and constraints, so clear,

00:11:10.890 --> 00:11:16.530
specific prompt makes a
measurable difference.

