let's align on a few terms
you'll see throughout

00:17:13.230 --> 00:17:15.120
Security Copilot.

00:17:15.120 --> 00:17:19.920
A session is a particular
conversation within Copilot,

00:17:19.920 --> 00:17:24.090
and Copilot maintains
context within that session.

00:17:24.090 --> 00:17:26.790
A prompt is a specific statement

00:17:26.790 --> 00:17:31.710
or question inside the session
entered through the prompt bar.

00:17:31.710 --> 00:17:37.350
A capability is a function
Copilot uses to solve part

00:17:37.350 --> 00:17:41.760
of a problem, and you may
also hear capability referred

00:17:41.760 --> 00:17:43.500
to as skills.

00:17:43.500 --> 00:17:47.880
A plugin is a collection
of capabilities provided

00:17:47.880 --> 00:17:52.320
by a particular resource,
like Microsoft Intune,

00:17:52.320 --> 00:17:55.800
and the orchestrator
is Copilot system

00:17:55.800 --> 00:18:00.960
for composing those capabilities
together to answer your prompt.

00:18:00.960 --> 00:18:05.280
at the center of the
experience is the prompt bar.

00:18:05.280 --> 00:18:08.820
You use it to tell Copilot
what insights you want

00:18:08.820 --> 00:18:11.070
from your security data.

00:18:11.070 --> 00:18:15.480
Behind the scene, Copilot
integrates with various sources

00:18:15.480 --> 00:18:19.380
through plugins, including
Microsoft Security product

00:18:19.380 --> 00:18:23.810
such as Microsoft Sentinel,
Microsoft Defender XDR,

00:18:23.810 --> 00:18:28.610
Microsoft Intune, as well
as non-Microsoft solution

00:18:28.610 --> 00:18:31.640
and Open Source
Intelligence feed.

00:18:31.640 --> 00:18:36.350
For each data source, the plugin
brings a set of capabilities,

00:18:36.350 --> 00:18:40.490
each one designed to
perform a specialized task

00:18:40.490 --> 00:18:44.190
within that product scope.

00:18:44.190 --> 00:18:46.140
Here's a concrete example.

00:18:46.140 --> 00:18:50.640
The plugin for Microsoft
Defender XDR supports capability

00:18:50.640 --> 00:18:55.470
such as analyzing a file,
generating an incident report,

00:18:55.470 --> 00:18:59.160
generating guided
response, listing incident

00:18:59.160 --> 00:19:01.290
and related alerts, as well

00:19:01.290 --> 00:19:05.310
as summarizing the security
state of a device.

00:19:05.310 --> 00:19:09.240
When you ask a question,
the orchestrator can choose

00:19:09.240 --> 00:19:12.330
and combine these capabilities

00:19:12.330 --> 00:19:16.830
so the response isn't just
some generic text answer.

00:19:16.830 --> 00:19:23.100
It's grounded in your
security tooling and data.

00:19:23.100 --> 00:19:26.040
Now let's make
prompt-writing practical.

00:19:26.040 --> 00:19:27.510
Security analysts

00:19:27.510 --> 00:19:32.010
and researchers should
include four elements

00:19:32.010 --> 00:19:36.570
when writing an effective
prompt: the goal, the context,

00:19:36.570 --> 00:19:39.630
the expectation, and the source.

00:19:39.630 --> 00:19:45.240
Your goal is the specific
security-related information

00:19:45.240 --> 00:19:46.470
you need.

00:19:46.470 --> 00:19:51.990
Context explain why you need
it or how you'll use it.

00:19:51.990 --> 00:19:56.340
Expectation defines the
format or target audience,

00:19:56.340 --> 00:19:59.820
like whether you want bullet
points, a short summary,

00:19:59.820 --> 00:20:03.480
or a report written
for executives.

00:20:03.480 --> 00:20:08.370
And source is the known
information data source

00:20:08.370 --> 00:20:12.240
or plugin Copilot should use.

00:20:12.240 --> 00:20:15.750
As you write prompt, keep
a few things in mind.

00:20:15.750 --> 00:20:19.170
Be specific and concise.

00:20:19.170 --> 00:20:23.370
Iterate. Try alternative
version of your prompt

00:20:23.370 --> 00:20:27.060
if the first response
isn't quite what you need.

00:20:27.060 --> 00:20:32.460
Provide necessary context to
narrow down where Copilot looks.

00:20:32.460 --> 00:20:36.870
Give positive instruction
instead of focusing on what not

00:20:36.870 --> 00:20:42.180
to do and directly
address Copilot as you

00:20:42.180 --> 00:20:45.730
to make the request
clear and actionable.

00:20:46.880 --> 00:20:48.950
Now let's walk
through what it takes

00:20:48.950 --> 00:20:52.430
to enable Microsoft
Security Copilot

00:20:52.430 --> 00:20:55.970
to start using the service
organization onboard both the

00:20:55.970 --> 00:20:58.550
service and the user.

00:20:58.550 --> 00:21:02.540
Practically, you navigate to
the Security Copilot site,

00:21:02.540 --> 00:21:05.300
choose an Azure
subscription, and choose

00:21:05.300 --> 00:21:08.810
or create a secure
resource group.

00:21:08.810 --> 00:21:13.640
Then you provision Copilot
capacity by naming your capacity

00:21:13.640 --> 00:21:19.070
and adding at least 1 SCU,
security compute unit.

00:21:19.070 --> 00:21:22.590
After that, you set up
the default environment

00:21:22.590 --> 00:21:25.710
and assign the appropriate
role permission.

00:21:25.710 --> 00:21:30.560
Each of these steps is
explored in more details next.

00:21:30.560 --> 00:21:34.970
Before anyone can begin using
Copilot, Administrators need

00:21:34.970 --> 00:21:38.000
to provision and
allocate capacity.

00:21:38.000 --> 00:21:40.560
You must have an
Azure subscription,

00:21:40.560 --> 00:21:45.870
and you must be an Azure owner,
or at least an Azure contributor

00:21:45.870 --> 00:21:48.060
at the resource group level.

00:21:48.060 --> 00:21:49.830
One important nuance is

00:21:49.830 --> 00:21:53.370
that Global Administrators
aren't automatically Azure

00:21:53.370 --> 00:21:56.520
owners or contributor
by default.

00:21:56.520 --> 00:22:01.290
That access can be configured
in the Azure portal.

00:22:01.290 --> 00:22:03.930
There are two ways to
provision capacity.

00:22:03.930 --> 00:22:08.400
You can provision capacity
within Security Copilot,

00:22:08.400 --> 00:22:10.410
which is the
recommended approach,

00:22:10.410 --> 00:22:15.240
or you can provision capacity
through the Azure portal.

00:22:15.240 --> 00:22:17.340
Once capacity is in place,

00:22:17.340 --> 00:22:22.380
Security Copilot also provides
a usage monitoring dashboard

00:22:22.380 --> 00:22:26.580
for capacity owners, so
you can track consumption

00:22:26.580 --> 00:22:31.170
and manage how
capacity is allocated.

00:22:31.170 --> 00:22:36.000
After provisioning capacity, you
set up the default environment.

00:22:36.000 --> 00:22:37.560
To do that, you'll need one

00:22:37.560 --> 00:22:41.760
of the following Microsoft Entra
ID role, Global Administrator,

00:22:41.760 --> 00:22:44.160
or Security Administrator.

00:22:44.160 --> 00:22:47.730
During setup, you're prompted
to configure several settings,

00:22:47.730 --> 00:22:51.810
including how much SCU,
security compute unit,

00:22:51.810 --> 00:22:56.220
capacity to allocate and
the geographic location

00:22:56.220 --> 00:22:59.700
of your tenant because
customer data collected

00:22:59.700 --> 00:23:04.020
by the service is
stored in that region.

00:23:04.020 --> 00:23:07.530
Security Copilot operates
in Azure Data Center

00:23:07.530 --> 00:23:11.160
in multiple regions,
including the European Union,

00:23:11.160 --> 00:23:16.560
United Kingdom, United States,
Australia, New Zealand, Japan,

00:23:16.560 --> 00:23:18.870
Canada, South America.

00:23:18.870 --> 00:23:22.830
You can also decide
whether to opt in or opt

00:23:22.830 --> 00:23:28.200
out of data-sharing option
for product improvements.

00:23:28.200 --> 00:23:32.640
For example, you can allow
Microsoft to capture data

00:23:32.640 --> 00:23:34.320
from Security Copilot

00:23:34.320 --> 00:23:38.280
and validate product
performance using human review

00:23:38.280 --> 00:23:43.080
or allow capture and human
review to help build

00:23:43.080 --> 00:23:46.710
and validate Microsoft
Security AI model.

00:23:46.710 --> 00:23:52.080
Importantly, opting in
doesn't allow Microsoft

00:23:52.080 --> 00:23:55.920
to use customer data to
train foundational model.

00:23:55.920 --> 00:24:00.480
Another key setting controls
whether Security Copilot can

00:24:00.480 --> 00:24:05.580
access data from your
Microsoft 365 services.

00:24:05.580 --> 00:24:08.370
If this option is turned off,

00:24:08.370 --> 00:24:11.880
your organization
can't use plugins

00:24:11.880 --> 00:24:16.110
that accesses Microsoft
365 services.

00:24:16.110 --> 00:24:18.870
And currently this
option is required

00:24:18.870 --> 00:24:21.960
for using the Microsoft
Purview plugin.

00:24:21.960 --> 00:24:25.170
Challenging it requires a user

00:24:25.170 --> 00:24:28.360
with a Global
Administrator role.

00:24:29.610 --> 00:24:31.710
Now let's clarify permission.

00:24:31.710 --> 00:24:36.450
You can assign access using
Microsoft Entra ID role

00:24:36.450 --> 00:24:40.590
or Security Copilot role,
and a best practice is

00:24:40.590 --> 00:24:42.840
to use the least-privileged role

00:24:42.840 --> 00:24:47.010
that still enables the
work someone needs to do.

00:24:47.010 --> 00:24:50.160
Microsoft Entra role like
Global Administrators,

00:24:50.160 --> 00:24:53.340
Security Administrators,
Security Operator,

00:24:53.340 --> 00:24:57.300
and Security Reader,
apply beyond Copilot,

00:24:57.300 --> 00:25:01.980
so they grant broader
permission across the tenant.

00:25:01.980 --> 00:25:07.440
To keep Copilot access more
targeted Security Copilot also

00:25:07.440 --> 00:25:12.150
introduces two roles that
function like access groups,

00:25:12.150 --> 00:25:17.130
but are managed within
Copilot itself: Copilot owner

00:25:17.130 --> 00:25:19.440
and Copilot contributor.

00:25:19.440 --> 00:25:25.320
These roles grant access only
to Security Copilot features.

00:25:25.320 --> 00:25:29.130
The Security Administrator and
Global Administrator roles

00:25:29.130 --> 00:25:35.160
in Microsoft Entra automatically
inherit Copilot owner access,

00:25:35.160 --> 00:25:40.680
and by default, all user and the
Entra tenant are given Copilot

00:25:40.680 --> 00:25:44.550
contributor access,
meaning any user

00:25:44.550 --> 00:25:48.030
with a licensed tenant
can create sessions

00:25:48.030 --> 00:25:53.070
and run prompt unless an
admin restricts what access

00:25:53.070 --> 00:25:57.510
by removing all users from the
workspace contributor assignment

00:25:57.510 --> 00:26:01.530
and replacing it with a
specific security group.

00:26:01.530 --> 00:26:05.910
One more nuance, Copilot doesn't
go beyond the access you've

00:26:05.910 --> 00:26:07.680
already have.

00:26:07.680 --> 00:26:12.240
Even if you can open
Copilot and create prompt,

00:26:12.240 --> 00:26:15.840
individual plugins may
require additional

00:26:15.840 --> 00:26:17.700
product-specific role.

00:26:17.700 --> 00:26:22.260
For example, to use
Microsoft Sentinel plugin,

00:26:22.260 --> 00:26:24.480
you need a sentinel role,

00:26:24.480 --> 00:26:29.040
like reader to access
incident in the workspace.

00:26:29.040 --> 00:26:32.970
To use the Intune plugin
for devices and policy,

00:26:32.970 --> 00:26:36.330
you need an
Intune-specific role,

00:26:36.330 --> 00:26:39.030
such as an Endpoint
Security Manager.

00:26:39.030 --> 00:26:45.630
In many cases, Microsoft plugin
uses an on-behalf-of model.

00:26:45.630 --> 00:26:51.150
Copilot, recognizes licensing
and signs into product

00:26:51.150 --> 00:26:55.050
on your behalf, whether
the plugin is enabled,

00:26:55.050 --> 00:26:57.990
while some plugin
requires extra setup

00:26:57.990 --> 00:27:01.150
and parameter for
authentication.

00:27:02.160 --> 00:27:04.500
Let's summarize this module.

00:27:04.500 --> 00:27:07.830
Microsoft Security
Copilot is an AI-powered,

00:27:07.830 --> 00:27:11.340
cloud-based security
analysis tool designed

00:27:11.340 --> 00:27:14.640
to help organization meet
the growing challenge

00:27:14.640 --> 00:27:16.620
of cybersecurity.

00:27:16.620 --> 00:27:21.240
You'll learn what Security
Copilot is, key terminology,

00:27:21.240 --> 00:27:24.270
how prompt requests are
processed through plugins

00:27:24.270 --> 00:27:28.710
and capabilities, what
makes a prompt effective,

00:27:28.710 --> 00:27:32.010
and the practical steps to
enable Security Copilot,

00:27:32.010 --> 00:27:37.810
including capacity, environment
setup, and the role permissions.

00:27:38.820 --> 00:27:42.870
Next, we'll focus on
embedded experiences

00:27:42.870 --> 00:27:46.920
where a Security Copilot
is accessible directly

00:27:46.920 --> 00:27:49.650
from Microsoft Security product.

00:27:49.650 --> 00:27:54.060
This matters because it
brings AI assistant right

00:27:54.060 --> 00:27:56.820
into the investigator's
workflow,

00:27:56.820 --> 00:28:02.410
reducing context-switching
and enabling faster decision.

00:28:04.170 --> 00:28:07.650
Microsoft Security Copilot
is accessible directly

00:28:07.650 --> 00:28:12.210
through some Microsoft Security
product, and that's what we mean

00:28:12.210 --> 00:28:14.310
by the embedded experience.

00:28:14.310 --> 00:28:17.340
In this module, we'll take a
look at the scenario supported

00:28:17.340 --> 00:28:21.810
by the embedded experience in
Microsoft Security solution.

00:28:21.810 --> 00:28:24.600
By the end of this
module, you'll be able

00:28:24.600 --> 00:28:29.460
to describe Microsoft Copilot
in Microsoft Defender XDR,

00:28:29.460 --> 00:28:33.970
Microsoft Purview,
and Microsoft Entra.

00:28:35.040 --> 00:28:39.780
Here we bring the embedded
concept into focus.

00:28:39.780 --> 00:28:43.260
Microsoft Security Copilot
is accessible directly

00:28:43.260 --> 00:28:47.640
from some Microsoft Security
product such as Defender XDR,

00:28:47.640 --> 00:28:53.550
Microsoft Entra, and Purview,
with more integration coming.

00:28:53.550 --> 00:28:55.770
In the embedded experience,

00:28:55.770 --> 00:29:00.510
Copilot invokes product-specific
capabilities directly,

00:29:00.510 --> 00:29:03.930
which can improve
processing efficiency

00:29:03.930 --> 00:29:06.120
because the right tool

00:29:06.120 --> 00:29:11.460
and context are immediately
available to Security Copilot.

00:29:11.460 --> 00:29:14.490
The embedded experience
is also a great place

00:29:14.490 --> 00:29:17.670
to start a security
investigation,

00:29:17.670 --> 00:29:22.590
and when you need a deeper
cross-product investigation,

00:29:22.590 --> 00:29:27.750
you can seamlessly transition
to the standalone experience

00:29:27.750 --> 00:29:32.010
to bring in all the Copilot
capabilities enabled

00:29:32.010 --> 00:29:33.720
for your role.

00:29:33.720 --> 00:29:37.410
To use an embedded experience,
the Microsoft plugin

00:29:37.410 --> 00:29:39.750
for that solution
must be enabled,

00:29:39.750 --> 00:29:43.830
and the user must have
permission to access Copilot,

00:29:43.830 --> 00:29:46.950
plus any additional
permission required

00:29:46.950 --> 00:29:51.480
to access the underlying
product and data.

00:29:51.480 --> 00:29:53.610
Let's take a look
at a key example.

00:29:53.610 --> 00:29:58.140
Security Copilot embeds in
Microsoft Defender XDR.

00:29:58.140 --> 00:30:03.780
This integration is designed to
help security team investigate

00:30:03.780 --> 00:30:08.580
and respond to incidents
quickly and efficiently.

00:30:08.580 --> 00:30:13.050
Within Defender XDR, Copilot
can support capabilities

00:30:13.050 --> 00:30:17.310
such as summarizing events,
providing guided responses,

00:30:17.310 --> 00:30:21.030
analyzing scripts,
translating natural language

00:30:21.030 --> 00:30:24.900
into KQL queries for
advanced hunting,

00:30:24.900 --> 00:30:29.070
generating incident
reports, analyzing file,

00:30:29.070 --> 00:30:33.330
and summarizing devices.

00:30:33.330 --> 00:30:36.630
When you need to understand
an incident fast,

00:30:36.630 --> 00:30:41.280
Copilot in Defender XDR can
generate an incident summary.

00:30:41.280 --> 00:30:44.460
Copilot creates an
overview of the attack

00:30:44.460 --> 00:30:47.520
that helps you quickly
understand what happened,

00:30:47.520 --> 00:30:51.900
what assets are involved, and
the timeline of the attack.

00:30:51.900 --> 00:30:55.740
In fact, Copilot automatically
creates a summary

00:30:55.740 --> 00:30:58.680
when you navigate to
an incident page.

00:30:58.680 --> 00:31:03.450
Incidents containing up to
100 alerts can be summarized

00:31:03.450 --> 00:31:06.810
into a single incident summary.

00:31:06.810 --> 00:31:11.850
Depending on the data available,
that summary can include

00:31:11.850 --> 00:31:15.330
when the attack started,
where it started --

00:31:15.330 --> 00:31:18.510
meaning the entity
or asset involved --

00:31:18.510 --> 00:31:22.950
a timeline of how the attack
unfolded, the assets involved,

00:31:22.950 --> 00:31:26.520
indicator of compromise,
and even the name

00:31:26.520 --> 00:31:29.250
of threat actors involved.

00:31:29.250 --> 00:31:31.260
The value is immediate.

00:31:31.260 --> 00:31:35.940
You spend less time
reading raw alert detail

00:31:35.940 --> 00:31:41.400
and more time making
informed-response decision.

00:31:41.400 --> 00:31:45.450
Another powerful capability
is guided responses.

00:31:45.450 --> 00:31:49.650
Copilot in Defender XDR uses
AI and machine learning

00:31:49.650 --> 00:31:53.100
to contextualize an
incident and learn

00:31:53.100 --> 00:31:55.620
from previous investigation,

00:31:55.620 --> 00:31:59.490
generating appropriate response
action you can follow.

00:31:59.490 --> 00:32:02.160
This helps incident
response team

00:32:02.160 --> 00:32:08.370
at all level apply response
actions confidently and quickly.

00:32:08.370 --> 00:32:11.940
Guided response are
organized into categories.

00:32:11.940 --> 00:32:16.260
Triage includes recommendation
for classifying incidents

00:32:16.260 --> 00:32:21.120
as informational, true
positive, false positive.

00:32:21.120 --> 00:32:25.740
Containment provides action
to contain the incident.

00:32:25.740 --> 00:32:30.930
Investigation suggests next
step to gather more evidence,

00:32:30.930 --> 00:32:34.740
and remediation, focuses
on response action applied

00:32:34.740 --> 00:32:38.700
to specific entities
involved in the incident.

00:32:38.700 --> 00:32:42.960
Each guided response
card includes details

00:32:42.960 --> 00:32:45.660
like why the action
is recommended,

00:32:45.660 --> 00:32:49.860
and may include options such
as viewing similar incident,

00:32:49.860 --> 00:32:54.240
helping you learn from patterns
across your environments.

00:32:54.240 --> 00:33:00.240
For remediation actions, team
can also view user information

00:33:00.240 --> 00:33:05.460
when steps like password
reset are required.

00:33:05.460 --> 00:33:09.750
Many sophisticated attack,
including ransomware, use script

00:33:09.750 --> 00:33:12.600
and PowerShell to
evade detection,

00:33:12.600 --> 00:33:15.690
and dual scripts are
often obfuscated,

00:33:15.690 --> 00:33:19.740
making analysis low
and error-prone.

00:33:19.740 --> 00:33:22.710
Copilot's script
analysis capability

00:33:22.710 --> 00:33:25.890
in Defender XDR helps
reduce that friction

00:33:25.890 --> 00:33:28.950
by letting you inspect
script and code

00:33:28.950 --> 00:33:32.010
without relying
on external tool.

00:33:32.010 --> 00:33:35.700
You can access script analysis
from the alert timeline

00:33:35.700 --> 00:33:39.780
within an incident specifically
for timeline entries

00:33:39.780 --> 00:33:45.480
that includes a script or code
such as PowerShell.exe event.

00:33:45.480 --> 00:33:47.880
Copilot support
scripts languages

00:33:47.880 --> 00:33:52.050
like PowerShell, Batch, Bash.

00:33:52.050 --> 00:33:54.870
The outcome is
faster understanding

00:33:54.870 --> 00:33:58.290
of what the script is doing
and quicker determination

00:33:58.290 --> 00:34:02.410
of whether it's
malicious or benign.

00:34:04.260 --> 00:34:09.120
Threat-hunting often requires
writing KQL queries,

00:34:09.120 --> 00:34:13.200
and that can be a barrier if
you're new to the language

00:34:13.200 --> 00:34:15.300
or you're trying
to move quickly.

00:34:15.300 --> 00:34:20.430
Copilot in Defender XDR includes
a query-assistant capability

00:34:20.430 --> 00:34:24.090
in advanced hunting that
reduces the time it takes

00:34:24.090 --> 00:34:26.670
to write hunting
queries from scratch.

00:34:26.670 --> 00:34:30.690
You can generate a KQL query
from a suggested prompt,

00:34:30.690 --> 00:34:34.080
or you can enter your own
prompt in natural language,

00:34:34.080 --> 00:34:38.850
like asking for all alerts
involving a specific user.

00:34:38.850 --> 00:34:42.780
Copilot then generates
the KQL query aligned

00:34:42.780 --> 00:34:45.810
to the advanced query schema.

00:34:45.810 --> 00:34:50.130
From there, you can run the
query directly or insert it

00:34:50.130 --> 00:34:56.510
in the editor to refine it, save
it, share it, or run it later.

00:34:56.510 --> 00:35:00.800
This frees you to focus on
investigating threats instead

00:35:00.800 --> 00:35:04.350
of wrestling with the syntax.

00:35:04.350 --> 00:35:08.940
Here we shift from
investigation to documentation.

00:35:08.940 --> 00:35:11.490
In Microsoft Defender XDR,

00:35:11.490 --> 00:35:14.850
Copilot can generate a
full incident report

00:35:14.850 --> 00:35:17.340
with just a single action.

00:35:17.340 --> 00:35:22.440
By selecting the ellipsis or the
report icon, you can ask Copilot

00:35:22.440 --> 00:35:26.850
to generate an incident report
that consolidates everything

00:35:26.850 --> 00:35:29.520
that happened during the
incident life cycle.

00:35:29.520 --> 00:35:34.320
That includes key timestamp for
incident management actions,

00:35:34.320 --> 00:35:38.310
the analysts involved
in the response,

00:35:38.310 --> 00:35:42.840
how the incident was classified,
and the investigation

00:35:42.840 --> 00:35:45.870
and remediation action
that were taken.

00:35:45.870 --> 00:35:48.090
It also captures
follow-up action,

00:35:48.090 --> 00:35:51.510
recommendation, and next steps.

00:35:51.510 --> 00:35:55.410
While an incident summary
gives you a fast overview

00:35:55.410 --> 00:36:00.390
of what happened, an incident
report provides a comprehensive,

00:36:00.390 --> 00:36:05.970
auditable record that brings
together analyst-driven steps,

00:36:05.970 --> 00:36:11.970
automated action, and supported
context from across Defender XDR

00:36:11.970 --> 00:36:14.590
and Microsoft Sentinel.

00:36:15.630 --> 00:36:20.220
Let's take a look at how Copilot
helps with file analysis.

00:36:20.220 --> 00:36:23.190
Sophisticated attacks
often rely on files

00:36:23.190 --> 00:36:29.780
that closely mimic legitimate or
system files to avoid detection.

00:36:29.780 --> 00:36:34.460
Copilot in Microsoft Defender
XDR enables security team

00:36:34.460 --> 00:36:39.020
to quickly identify
suspicious or malicious file

00:36:39.020 --> 00:36:42.930
without manually digging
into raw details.

00:36:42.930 --> 00:36:46.950
When you analyze a file,
Copilot produces an overview

00:36:46.950 --> 00:36:50.910
that assesses the file's
behavior and risk level,

00:36:50.910 --> 00:36:53.970
along with a detailed
section that includes things

00:36:53.970 --> 00:37:00.360
like embedded strings, API call,
and relevant certificates.

00:37:00.360 --> 00:37:05.250
This allows analysts to move
quickly from uncertainty

00:37:05.250 --> 00:37:09.390
to clarity and decide whether
the file represents a

00:37:09.390 --> 00:37:11.760
real threat.

00:37:11.760 --> 00:37:16.080
Another valuable capability
is device summarization.

00:37:16.080 --> 00:37:18.720
Copilot can generate
a concise summary

00:37:18.720 --> 00:37:22.950
of a device security posture,
including vulnerable software,

00:37:22.950 --> 00:37:26.670
exposure level, and
any unusual behavior

00:37:26.670 --> 00:37:28.320
that have been observed.

00:37:28.320 --> 00:37:31.440
Security analysts can
use this device summary

00:37:31.440 --> 00:37:33.420
to accelerate investigation

00:37:33.420 --> 00:37:36.300
by understanding
the broader context

00:37:36.300 --> 00:37:39.120
of a device involved
in an incident.

00:37:39.120 --> 00:37:43.440
Whether accessed directly
from the incident assets view

00:37:43.440 --> 00:37:46.050
or from the device
overview page,

00:37:46.050 --> 00:37:50.340
the summary helps team quickly
determine whether a device

00:37:50.340 --> 00:37:55.020
represents a larger risk
to the environment.

00:37:55.020 --> 00:37:58.710
Across all these
Defender XDR feature,

00:37:58.710 --> 00:38:02.130
there's a few capabilities
that remain consistent.

00:38:02.130 --> 00:38:04.410
One is feedback.

00:38:04.410 --> 00:38:07.410
Just like the
standalone experience,

00:38:07.410 --> 00:38:10.830
the embedded experience allows
you to provide feedback

00:38:10.830 --> 00:38:15.870
on AI-generated responses,
whether they look accurate,

00:38:15.870 --> 00:38:20.010
off target, or potentially
inappropriate.

00:38:20.010 --> 00:38:24.120
This feedback helps improve
the overall quality

00:38:24.120 --> 00:38:28.920
and reliability of
Copilot responses.

00:38:28.920 --> 00:38:32.490
Another shared capability
is the ability to move

00:38:32.490 --> 00:38:37.170
from the embedded experience
into the standalone experience.

00:38:37.170 --> 00:38:41.940
The embedded Copilot is a great
place to start an investigation,

00:38:41.940 --> 00:38:47.460
especially when you're already
working inside the vendor XDR.

00:38:47.460 --> 00:38:49.890
If your investigation expands

00:38:49.890 --> 00:38:53.460
and requires deeper
cross-product analysis,

00:38:53.460 --> 00:38:58.410
you can seamlessly transition
into the standalone experience

00:38:58.410 --> 00:39:00.210
and continue with the full set

00:39:00.210 --> 00:39:05.460
of Copilot capabilities
enabled for your role.

00:39:05.460 --> 00:39:09.090
Now let's introduce
another powerful extension

00:39:09.090 --> 00:39:15.210
of Copilot Security: Copilot
agents and Microsoft Defender.

00:39:15.210 --> 00:39:18.930
These agents are available
through the security store

00:39:18.930 --> 00:39:23.160
in the Defender portal and
are designed to help automate

00:39:23.160 --> 00:39:28.530
and accelerate common
security operation task.

00:39:28.530 --> 00:39:31.080
In the Microsoft
defender portal.

00:39:31.080 --> 00:39:33.630
The security store offers
a growing collection

00:39:33.630 --> 00:39:37.170
of agents published by
Microsoft and partners.

00:39:37.170 --> 00:39:41.250
These agents integrate directly
into Microsoft Defender

00:39:41.250 --> 00:39:46.290
and perform tasks such as
incident triage, investigation,

00:39:46.290 --> 00:39:49.920
threat-hunting, and
threat intelligence.

00:39:49.920 --> 00:39:54.420
Each agent is purpose-built
to reduce manual effort

00:39:54.420 --> 00:39:59.210
and helps SOC team
operate more efficiently.

00:39:59.210 --> 00:40:01.310
By the end of this
module, you'll be able

00:40:01.310 --> 00:40:04.790
to describe the Security
Copilot AI agents available

00:40:04.790 --> 00:40:06.560
in Microsoft Defender

00:40:06.560 --> 00:40:10.610
and understand how they
support real-world security

00:40:10.610 --> 00:40:13.170
operation workflow.

00:40:14.460 --> 00:40:18.380
Let's take a closer look at the
agents currently available.

00:40:18.380 --> 00:40:21.590
To purchase and deploy custom
agent from the security store,

00:40:21.590 --> 00:40:26.210
you need access to the Security
Copilot workspace provisioned

00:40:26.210 --> 00:40:29.180
with SCU capability.

00:40:29.180 --> 00:40:34.100
The phishing triage agent helps
security analysts automatically

00:40:34.100 --> 00:40:38.130
triage and classify
user-submitted

00:40:38.130 --> 00:40:39.750
phishing incident.

00:40:39.750 --> 00:40:42.840
It operates autonomously
and is triggered

00:40:42.840 --> 00:40:46.020
when a user reports
phishing emails.

00:40:46.020 --> 00:40:50.550
The agent requires Microsoft
Defender for Endpoint Plan 2

00:40:50.550 --> 00:40:54.600
and Security Administrator
permission to configure.

00:40:54.600 --> 00:40:58.260
The threat-intelligence briefing
agent provides regular,

00:40:58.260 --> 00:41:01.590
customized threat-intelligence
briefing by gathering

00:41:01.590 --> 00:41:05.700
and synthesizing information
from multiple sources.

00:41:05.700 --> 00:41:09.000
It runs autonomously and
helps teams stay focused

00:41:09.000 --> 00:41:13.050
about relevant threat
without manual research.

00:41:13.050 --> 00:41:16.230
The threat-hunting
agents assist analysts

00:41:16.230 --> 00:41:20.160
with end-to-end threat-hunting
using natural language.

00:41:20.160 --> 00:41:23.610
It not only generates
KQL queries,

00:41:23.610 --> 00:41:27.750
but also interprets
results, surfaces insight,

00:41:27.750 --> 00:41:32.730
and guides analysts through
a complete hunting session.

00:41:32.730 --> 00:41:37.920
Finally, the Dynamics Threat
Detection agent is an always-on

00:41:37.920 --> 00:41:41.250
backend service that
continuously looks

00:41:41.250 --> 00:41:45.870
for hidden threats across
Defender and Microsoft Sentinel.

00:41:45.870 --> 00:41:51.390
It uses AI to correlate
alerts, events, anomaly,

00:41:51.390 --> 00:41:53.160
and threat intelligence

00:41:53.160 --> 00:41:58.830
to uncover false negative
and detection gaps.

00:41:58.830 --> 00:42:02.310
Here we focus on the phishing
triage agent in action.

00:42:02.310 --> 00:42:05.790
Once deployed, this agent
automatically evaluates

00:42:05.790 --> 00:42:10.260
user-submitted phishing
messages, analyzes indicators,

00:42:10.260 --> 00:42:12.810
and classifies the incident.

00:42:12.810 --> 00:42:17.940
By offloading routine phishing
analysis to an autonomous agent,

00:42:17.940 --> 00:42:22.290
SOC team can reduce alert
fatigue and focus their time

00:42:22.290 --> 00:42:25.380
on higher-impact investigation.

