It uses large language models,
so there's no form or structure

00:52:20.610 --> 00:52:23.970
that you're expected
to use in your prompt.

00:52:23.970 --> 00:52:29.220
Express yourself using natural
language, and take a look

00:52:29.220 --> 00:52:32.850
at the predefined prompt
to get ideas of the type

00:52:32.850 --> 00:52:35.970
of interaction, natural
language interaction,

00:52:35.970 --> 00:52:40.120
we can have with
Security Copilot.

00:52:41.520 --> 00:52:43.740
For example, here's a prompt.

00:52:43.740 --> 00:52:46.710
What group is app
name assigned to?

00:52:46.710 --> 00:52:50.550
What are the critical
Sentinel incident right now?

00:52:50.550 --> 00:52:52.470
We can click on those.

00:52:52.470 --> 00:52:56.400
If we select, for example, what
group is app name assigned to,

00:52:56.400 --> 00:52:58.170
app name would be a parameter.

00:52:58.170 --> 00:53:03.120
We would enter the name of
the app we want to look at.

00:53:03.120 --> 00:53:05.550
All right, let's open
up a prompt book.

00:53:05.550 --> 00:53:08.490
Let's see promptbooks in action.

00:53:08.490 --> 00:53:10.860
Here, a promptbook
is a collection

00:53:10.860 --> 00:53:14.490
of predefined prompt
that will execute.

00:53:14.490 --> 00:53:16.170
They're usually parameterized.

00:53:16.170 --> 00:53:20.040
In this case, I would specify
the name of an actor,

00:53:20.040 --> 00:53:22.860
and then every single
prompt that we see

00:53:22.860 --> 00:53:26.100
in that promptbook
would be executed,

00:53:26.100 --> 00:53:29.770
and we'll see an example
of that later on.

00:53:31.920 --> 00:53:33.360
There's a number of promptbooks.

00:53:33.360 --> 00:53:35.880
I highly encourage
you to explore those.

00:53:35.880 --> 00:53:40.590
They're categorized by
roles like SOC Analyst,

00:53:40.590 --> 00:53:45.360
IT Administrator, Data
Security Administrator.

00:53:45.360 --> 00:53:48.850
This will filter
your promptbooks.

00:53:51.570 --> 00:53:53.700
And going back to sessions.

00:53:53.700 --> 00:53:55.260
This is very valuable.

00:53:55.260 --> 00:53:59.940
If you are handling multiple
cases at once, don't put them

00:53:59.940 --> 00:54:03.330
on the same conversation;
start new session.

00:54:03.330 --> 00:54:06.030
Let's open a previous session.

00:54:06.030 --> 00:54:08.130
Here's the conversation
that we had

00:54:08.130 --> 00:54:11.100
with Security
Copilot previously,

00:54:11.100 --> 00:54:17.250
where we asked what are the
last three failed login?

00:54:17.250 --> 00:54:19.710
And we can see the last
three failed logins,

00:54:19.710 --> 00:54:22.740
starting with Gebhard over here.

00:54:22.740 --> 00:54:27.390
Same user again and
same user again,

00:54:27.390 --> 00:54:30.420
three times the user
failed to log in.

00:54:30.420 --> 00:54:33.120
And now we're going to
ask what are the UPNs

00:54:33.120 --> 00:54:34.680
for the failed login?

00:54:34.680 --> 00:54:38.040
And Copilot gives us
a nice bulleted list

00:54:38.040 --> 00:54:40.230
of those user principal names.

00:54:40.230 --> 00:54:43.230
So use natural
language, ask question,

00:54:43.230 --> 00:54:47.670
get it to generate
a response for you.

00:54:47.670 --> 00:54:50.220
And from there, you can put
those in a spreadsheet.

00:54:50.220 --> 00:54:54.150
You can ask Copilot to give you
a table, to give you bullet,

00:54:54.150 --> 00:54:57.960
to give you whatever
output you'd like to use.

00:54:57.960 --> 00:55:00.330
This has been an overview

00:55:00.330 --> 00:55:04.470
of the interactive experience
of Security Copilot.

00:55:04.470 --> 00:55:06.270
Now let's take a look

00:55:06.270 --> 00:55:11.260
at the embedded experience
of Security Copilot.

00:55:13.830 --> 00:55:17.970
So we looked at the interactive
Copilot experience.

00:55:17.970 --> 00:55:21.030
Now let's flip things
around and take a look

00:55:21.030 --> 00:55:25.110
at the embedded experience,
where now Copilot kicks

00:55:25.110 --> 00:55:29.100
in automatically when you're
doing your investigation,

00:55:29.100 --> 00:55:31.440
when you're working in Defender.

00:55:31.440 --> 00:55:34.860
Let's take a look at an incident
that we have over here.

00:55:34.860 --> 00:55:38.460
Human-operated ransomware
attack was launched.

00:55:38.460 --> 00:55:40.200
That is concerning.

00:55:40.200 --> 00:55:42.900
Let's do a little bit
of investigation.

00:55:42.900 --> 00:55:46.530
As we open the incident,
notice on our right.

00:55:46.530 --> 00:55:51.690
Copilot comes in and immediately
provides a nice summary

00:55:51.690 --> 00:55:53.700
of the incident.

00:55:53.700 --> 00:55:54.240
There it is.

00:55:54.240 --> 00:55:57.150
I'm not going to read through
the whole thing, but very handy,

00:55:57.150 --> 00:56:02.580
including the attack story,
as well as suggestion on how

00:56:02.580 --> 00:56:07.590
to triage it, what to do
with our users, the entities

00:56:07.590 --> 00:56:12.240
that encountered compromise over
here, like contact this user,

00:56:12.240 --> 00:56:16.080
classify this incident.

00:56:16.080 --> 00:56:20.220
For now, we'll just mark the
incident as "Completed,"

00:56:20.220 --> 00:56:23.820
and let's continue to use
the embedded experience

00:56:23.820 --> 00:56:27.660
to see what Copilot
can do for us.

00:56:27.660 --> 00:56:30.180
We're going to take a
look at a specific alert,

00:56:30.180 --> 00:56:35.460
"Suspicious RDP session," and as
we select the alert, once again,

00:56:35.460 --> 00:56:40.570
Copilot kicks in and provides
a nice alert summary,

00:56:41.970 --> 00:56:48.060
including the concerning lateral
movement we're seeing over here.

00:56:48.060 --> 00:56:50.910
Let's take a look at the
alert in more detail.

00:56:50.910 --> 00:56:53.640
We're going to open
up the Alert page.

00:56:53.640 --> 00:56:55.200
We can see the process tree.

00:56:55.200 --> 00:56:58.260
We can see the device
that was involved.

00:56:58.260 --> 00:57:04.050
Let's use Copilot to provide
a summary of that device.

00:57:04.050 --> 00:57:05.010
Here we go.

00:57:05.010 --> 00:57:08.880
Let's expand it, and
here's the summary

00:57:08.880 --> 00:57:12.460
that was provided
to us by Copilot.

00:57:13.980 --> 00:57:15.810
We'll close it.

00:57:15.810 --> 00:57:20.490
We can do the same with the
user account, Jonathan Wolcott.

00:57:20.490 --> 00:57:25.650
Let's summarize their
information, and once again,

00:57:25.650 --> 00:57:29.020
there it is, coming
straight from Copilot.

00:57:32.700 --> 00:57:38.670
Let's close that, and let's take
a look at the process tree,

00:57:38.670 --> 00:57:42.540
see what triggered the alert.

00:57:42.540 --> 00:57:46.650
We'll expand it, scroll
down a little bit and, ooh,

00:57:46.650 --> 00:57:49.110
there's some PowerShell
stuff going on over here.

00:57:49.110 --> 00:57:52.350
We can see that PowerShell
executed a script.

00:57:52.350 --> 00:57:53.550
That kind of concerns me.

00:57:53.550 --> 00:57:55.440
That's an end-user machine.

00:57:55.440 --> 00:57:59.520
Why would they be executing
a PowerShell script?

00:57:59.520 --> 00:58:02.430
Here's the script
that got executed.

00:58:02.430 --> 00:58:04.830
Now, if you know how to
read partial code, cool.

00:58:04.830 --> 00:58:07.800
If you don't know how to read
partial code, not a problem.

00:58:07.800 --> 00:58:12.030
Let's get Copilot to
analyze the script for us.

00:58:12.030 --> 00:58:14.580
Here it is in plain
English for us

00:58:14.580 --> 00:58:19.410
to review step-by-step
what the script does.

00:58:19.410 --> 00:58:21.870
And what's beautiful
over here is even

00:58:21.870 --> 00:58:27.000
if the script was obfuscated,
Copilot would de-obfuscate it

00:58:27.000 --> 00:58:29.820
for us, explain to
us what it does,

00:58:29.820 --> 00:58:33.720
if there is any
concerning activities.

00:58:33.720 --> 00:58:37.650
Here it is line by line,
explained by Copilot.

00:58:37.650 --> 00:58:40.980
We can see the lines
of the script,

00:58:40.980 --> 00:58:44.400
and we can also see the MITRE
techniques that were used

00:58:44.400 --> 00:58:48.030
in the script, things
like native API call.

00:58:48.030 --> 00:58:52.620
Or below that, we've got a
system information discovery.

00:58:52.620 --> 00:58:55.140
So I don't need to be
able to read the script.

00:58:55.140 --> 00:58:59.130
Copilot does the heavy lifting
for me, so me at my end

00:58:59.130 --> 00:59:04.890
as a Security Operation Analyst,
I can now properly action

00:59:04.890 --> 00:59:08.590
and investigate that incident.

00:59:12.510 --> 00:59:17.430
Another thing Copilot can help
us with is understand files,

00:59:17.430 --> 00:59:18.960
not just scripts, but files

00:59:18.960 --> 00:59:21.120
that might have been
used in the attack.

00:59:21.120 --> 00:59:23.220
Let's take a look at that.

00:59:23.220 --> 00:59:27.720
Let's crack "Evidence and
Response" open, "Files."

00:59:27.720 --> 00:59:30.870
These are suspicious files
on the user's machine,

00:59:30.870 --> 00:59:32.400
starting with mimikatz.

00:59:32.400 --> 00:59:34.530
What the heck is mimikatz?

00:59:34.530 --> 00:59:36.180
Well, let's crack it open,

00:59:36.180 --> 00:59:39.960
and let's get Copilot
to explain it to us.

00:59:39.960 --> 00:59:41.430
Let's click on the ellipsis --

00:59:41.430 --> 00:59:51.990
-- print the file, and
generating a file analysis,

00:59:51.990 --> 00:59:53.760
and Copilot tells us the file,

00:59:53.760 --> 00:59:59.640
mimikatz.exe has been
detected, and it is a malware.

00:59:59.640 --> 01:00:01.500
It is a tool that is used

01:00:01.500 --> 01:00:05.430
to circumvent security
capture information.

01:00:05.430 --> 01:00:07.470
Copilot gives us that.

01:00:07.470 --> 01:00:10.440
So let's continue
our investigation.

01:00:10.440 --> 01:00:15.660
Now let's do a bit of KQL.

01:00:15.660 --> 01:00:21.120
Whether you're a KQL guru,
or you're entirely new,

01:00:21.120 --> 01:00:27.090
let's switch over to the
interactive Security

01:00:27.090 --> 01:00:30.120
Copilot experience.

01:00:30.120 --> 01:00:32.850
And what we're going to do
over here is we're going

01:00:32.850 --> 01:00:37.680
to ask Copilot to help us
further with that investigation,

01:00:37.680 --> 01:00:42.990
using conversational
plain language over here.

01:00:42.990 --> 01:00:46.800
So instead of us having to
figure everything out ourselves,

01:00:46.800 --> 01:00:52.140
let's use a promptbook to
kickstart our investigation.

01:00:52.140 --> 01:00:56.130
We're going to use the
Microsoft 365 Defender

01:00:56.130 --> 01:00:59.400
incident investigation.

01:00:59.400 --> 01:01:01.800
There's one parameter
we need to provide,

01:01:01.800 --> 01:01:04.800
and that's the
Defender incident ID.

01:01:04.800 --> 01:01:10.620
That was 185856, if I
remember correctly, "Submit."

01:01:10.620 --> 01:01:15.270
Now Security Copilot in the
interactive experience will pull

01:01:15.270 --> 01:01:21.040
up all the details that I need
to investigate that incident.

01:01:22.800 --> 01:01:26.700
There it is, lots of
actionable detail,

01:01:26.700 --> 01:01:29.230
still parsing through it.

01:01:30.990 --> 01:01:34.110
Now, I'm seeing some really
interesting stuff in here,

01:01:34.110 --> 01:01:36.990
stuff I want to make
sure I keep an eye on.

01:01:36.990 --> 01:01:40.950
So I'll select the result,
and I'll pin them,

01:01:40.950 --> 01:01:44.250
so now they're available on
the right side over here.

01:01:44.250 --> 01:01:48.330
I can pin evidence and
information that's related

01:01:48.330 --> 01:01:53.400
to my investigation and share
that evidence with my team

01:01:53.400 --> 01:01:56.760
as well, so we can
work collaboratively

01:01:56.760 --> 01:01:58.740
to handle this incident.

01:01:58.740 --> 01:02:00.510
I'm not going to
share it right now,

01:02:00.510 --> 01:02:04.680
but there's the "Share"
button for you.

01:02:04.680 --> 01:02:11.220
And finally, let's use
Security Copilot to help us

01:02:11.220 --> 01:02:15.570
with our query,
help us with KQL.

01:02:15.570 --> 01:02:18.990
KQL requires a bit of
specialized knowledge to be able

01:02:18.990 --> 01:02:21.960
to write a query, and we
are going to take a look

01:02:21.960 --> 01:02:25.920
at KQL later on in this course.

01:02:25.920 --> 01:02:29.130
But for now, let's get Copilot

01:02:29.130 --> 01:02:34.470
to help us generate a KQL
query using natural language.

01:02:34.470 --> 01:02:34.950
There we go.

01:02:34.950 --> 01:02:39.390
Based on the incident, create
a query to proactively hunt

01:02:39.390 --> 01:02:42.430
for the type of malware attack.

01:02:43.590 --> 01:02:44.820
Let's submit the prompt --

01:02:44.820 --> 01:02:55.390
-- and let's get Copilot to
generate a query for us.

01:03:01.530 --> 01:03:04.110
Shouldn't take too, too long.

01:03:04.110 --> 01:03:05.970
And there we go.

01:03:05.970 --> 01:03:09.450
Here is the query that
Copilot generated

01:03:09.450 --> 01:03:11.970
for us, fully documented.

01:03:11.970 --> 01:03:14.130
It may seem intimidating.

01:03:14.130 --> 01:03:17.550
If you want ask Copilot to
explain it to you further,

01:03:17.550 --> 01:03:21.180
line by line, but for
now, let's try it out.

01:03:21.180 --> 01:03:25.840
Let's go to "Hunting," or
let's just go to logs.

01:03:27.090 --> 01:03:29.340
Drop into query that
was generated for us

01:03:29.340 --> 01:03:33.160
by Copilot and run it.

01:03:34.530 --> 01:03:36.300
And here's our results.

01:03:36.300 --> 01:03:40.140
So we're able to generate
a sophisticated query

01:03:40.140 --> 01:03:47.250
without having to write a
single line of KQL code.

01:03:47.250 --> 01:03:50.370
If you need further
detail on the query,

01:03:50.370 --> 01:03:53.970
you need follow-up query,
you need to understand

01:03:53.970 --> 01:03:56.490
or change the query, go back

01:03:56.490 --> 01:04:00.870
to the interactive Copilot
experience and get Copilot

01:04:00.870 --> 01:04:05.970
to help you modify your
query, add to it, modify --

01:04:05.970 --> 01:04:08.760
whatever you need to do.

01:04:08.760 --> 01:04:11.280
Now that we've seen
the interactive

01:04:11.280 --> 01:04:15.810
and embedded experience
of Security Copilot,

01:04:15.810 --> 01:04:20.910
we've seen how we can use
generative AI in our workflow

01:04:20.910 --> 01:04:26.580
to help us action and
understand events, alerts,

01:04:26.580 --> 01:04:29.860
incidents in Sentinel.

01:04:31.200 --> 01:04:33.390
Let's wrap up Learning Path 2.

01:04:33.390 --> 01:04:34.680
In this learning path,

01:04:34.680 --> 01:04:38.790
you explored how Microsoft
Security Copilot is accessible

01:04:38.790 --> 01:04:41.790
directly from Microsoft
Security product

01:04:41.790 --> 01:04:44.220
through embedded experiences.

01:04:44.220 --> 01:04:47.640
You learned how to describe
Microsoft Security Copilot

01:04:47.640 --> 01:04:51.810
in Microsoft Defender
XDR, Microsoft Purview,

01:04:51.810 --> 01:04:57.000
and Microsoft Entra, and how
these integration helps security

01:04:57.000 --> 01:05:02.500
team investigate and respond
to threats more efficiently.

01:05:03.780 --> 01:05:05.700
This concludes our session.

01:05:05.700 --> 01:05:09.000
There's many ways to continue
your learning journey.

01:05:09.000 --> 01:05:13.110
We encourage you to watch other
videos in this course or search

01:05:13.110 --> 01:05:20.470
out your next favorite topic on
Microsoft Learn at aka.ms/Learn.

